{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504425,"nonce":"lkKWDswZI0kebT+j5vQ10sOb8Yo=","target":"539bfdd306f280b68f747a191c6ce710deb5b0ab92fa74d2c27a9049a1f0a10e","message":"**What is wrong.** The processing path is deliberately built to be replayed, and nothing can trigger a replay. `ingest.process_batch` is enqueued from exactly one place in the codebase — `internal/ingestion/service/accept.go:228`, inside the accept transaction that first received the payload. `cmd/oto` accepts six subcommands and none of them is a replay: `serve` (the default), `api`, `worker`, `migrate`, `bootstrap`, `version` (`cmd/oto/main.go:178-197`). No `operationId` in `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` mentions replay. The only way a stored batch is ever processed is the transaction that accepted it.\n\n**The demonstration.** The idempotency is finished, documented work. `internal/ingestion/worker/process_batch.go:33-44`: *\"SAFELY RE-RUNNABLE, which is not optional … Every write underneath is idempotent by construction (§G.5) — the alert upsert is ON CONFLICT, event appends dedupe through `alert_event_keys` — so even a batch left `partial` by a dead worker can be replayed wholesale.\"* `internal/ingestion/domain/batch.go:87` defines `Resumable()` as `pending || partial`, with the reasoning at `batch.go:85-86`: *\"`processed` and `failed` are the two genuinely terminal states.\"* That method is called from exactly one place, `internal/ingestion/service/process.go:63`, reachable only from the job handler.\n\nSo a `partial` batch is already replayable by a mechanism nothing can invoke, and a `failed` batch is not replayable at all. `markFailed` (`internal/ingestion/service/process.go:307-316`) writes `failed` and there is no un-fail path: `BatchRepository.MarkProcessed` (`internal/ingestion/repository/batches.go:181-184`) explicitly refuses the reverse transition — *\"a batch cannot be moved back to pending\"*. `failed` has exactly one legitimate exit today, and it is none.\n\n**Why it matters.** SPEC AC-36, `docs/design/SPEC.md:3840`: *\"Replaying a stored `ingest_batch` after a parser fix reproduces the same state without duplicate Slack messages.\"* The UI actively sells the capability to the operator as a reason to pay for disk. `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:850` warns that lowering `raw_retention_days` costs *\"the ability to reproduce an ingestion bug from the payload that caused it, and to replay a stored batch after a parser fix.\"* That copy asks an operator to retain bytes for a recovery action the binary cannot perform. Meanwhile a parser bug costs every alert in every affected batch, permanently, with Alertmanager's retry budget already spent — which is the exact loss `ingest_rejections` and the 202-never-4xx rule exist to prevent.\n\nThe constraint on a replay is already reasoned out in the same file. `tuningCopy.ts:854`: *\"Past thirty days a stored batch can no longer be replayed safely anyway — oto's event-dedupe keys have aged out, so re-processing it would append the timeline a second time.\"* That is precisely the check the entry point has to make before enqueueing.\n\n**Recommendation: `oto replay --batch \u003cid\u003e`, not an endpoint.** This is an operator recovery action taken after a code fix ships, not a tenant capability. It crosses the org boundary the API is scoped by — whoever fixed the parser does not know which org's batches broke, and `ResolveOrg` exists (`internal/ingestion/repository/batches.go:195`) precisely because a batch id carries no scope. It needs no idempotency-key story, no authz surface, and no place in the tenant contract. Exposing it over HTTP means adding an operation, a DTO, TS types and valibot validators through gates G1–G4 for something a tenant must never be able to call, and creates a route that re-enqueues work on the ingest pool from the outside. `cmd/oto` already builds the full container for `bootstrap`, so the wiring is there. Nothing in the tree argues the other way.\n\n**Where.** `internal/ingestion/service/accept.go:228` (the only enqueue), `internal/ingestion/service/process.go:63` (the only `Resumable()` call) and `:307-316` (`markFailed`), `internal/ingestion/repository/batches.go:181-184` (no un-fail path), `internal/ingestion/domain/batch.go:85-87`, `cmd/oto/main.go:178-197` (six subcommands, no replay), `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:850` (the copy that sells it), `docs/design/SPEC.md:3840` (AC-36).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** `oto replay --batch \u003cid\u003e` re-enqueues `ingest.process_batch` for a `failed` or `partial` batch, refuses with a message naming the horizon when the batch's `received_at` predates the `ingest_dedup` window rather than replaying into a duplicated timeline, and gives `failed` its one legitimate transition out. An integration test replays the same batch twice and asserts no second occurrence and no second Slack message, and `tuningCopy.ts:850`'s promise is true.","files":null}]}